Package: AgriDesign
Title: Design and Analysis of Agricultural Experiments
Version: 1.0.0
Authors@R:
    person("Luis Ángel", "Barrera-Guzmán", , "lbarrerag@chapingo.mx", role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-8057-2583"))
Description: Plans and analyses agricultural experiments with classical
    designs: completely randomised, randomised complete blocks (also
    generalised), Latin, Graeco-Latin and Youden squares, balanced, cyclic,
    alpha (Patterson and Williams (1976) <doi:10.1093/biomet/63.1.83>) and
    lattice incomplete blocks, augmented designs, factorials, split-plots,
    strip-plots, split-split-plots and nested designs, with or without
    covariates. Each analysis of variance tests every term against its own
    error stratum, with Satterthwaite (1946) <doi:10.2307/3002019> degrees
    of freedom for pooled errors; least-squares means are separated with
    eleven multiple-comparison procedures, among them Dunnett (1955)
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1955.10501294>, and compact letters, with simple
    effects, trends and contrasts. It checks the assumptions, offers
    transformations and rank-based alternatives, recovers inter-block
    information by restricted maximum likelihood (Patterson and Thompson
    (1971) <doi:10.1093/biomet/58.3.545>), randomises field layouts with
    field books and power calculations, draws publication figures and
    writes HTML reports. It also includes plant breeding analyses
    (additive main effects and multiplicative interaction, Gollob (1968)
    <doi:10.1007/BF02289676>; stability, Eberhart and Russell (1966)
    <doi:10.2135/cropsci1966.0011183X000600010011x>; line by tester;
    diallel, Griffing (1956) <doi:10.1071/BI9560463>; North Carolina
    designs) and plant pathology tools (area under the disease progress
    curve and stairs, Simko and Piepho (2012) <doi:10.1094/PHYTO-07-11-0216>;
    soil heterogeneity). Only base R is used.
